    <body>
      <sec id="article-43019.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Nitrogen dioxide is a highly toxic, reddish-brown oxidizing gas and a prominent air pollutant that primarily injures the respiratory tract. Produced during high-temperature combustion from motor vehicles, gas stoves, power plants, and agricultural silos, exposure occurs most often in enclosed or poorly ventilated environments. Occupational settings and confined-space incidents represent major risk factors. As a potent oxidant, nitrogen dioxide generates reactive nitrogen species that disrupt epithelial integrity, impair mucociliary clearance, and trigger inflammatory cascades, resulting in increased alveolar-capillary permeability.</p>
        <p>Clinical manifestations range from cough, throat irritation, and dyspnea to delayed-onset noncardiogenic pulmonary edema and acute lung injury. Diagnosis is clinical and exposure-based, supported by hypoxemia on pulse oximetry or arterial blood gas analysis and radiographic evidence of pulmonary infiltrates when severe. Management is supportive and includes prompt removal from exposure, supplemental oxygen, bronchodilators as indicated, and close observation for delayed deterioration. Complications include respiratory failure and bronchiolitis obliterans. Prognosis depends on exposure intensity and the timeliness of supportive care.</p>
        <p>This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance learners' competence in evaluating and managing nitrogen toxicity. Participants will advance their mastery of the condition's etiology, risk factors, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and evidence-based diagnostic and therapeutic approaches. Improved skills will equip clinicians to collaborate effectively with interprofessional teams&#x000a0;caring for affected individuals.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Assess patients for&#x000a0;nitrogen dioxide toxicity&#x000a0;based on clinical features and diagnostic findings.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based, individualized strategies for managing&#x000a0;nitrogen dioxide toxicity and mitigating its potential sequelae.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Improve patient awareness of safe ventilation, appliance use, confined-space precautions, and protective measures to minimize nitrogen dioxide exposure and related pulmonary complications.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the interprofessional team to educate, treat, and monitor patients at risk for&#x000a0;nitrogen dioxide toxicity to improve&#x000a0;health outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=43019&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=43019">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Nitrogen is a colorless, tasteless, and odorless gas comprising approximately 78% of air by volume.&#x000a0;This substance exists primarily as an inert diatomic molecule with no direct physiologic toxicity. Nitrogen oxides, including nitric oxide and nitrogen dioxide, are gases generated from nitrous or nitric acid as byproducts of reactions between nitric acid and organic materials, burning of nitrocellulose, detonations, and decomposition of rocket fuel.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r2">[2]</xref> Exposure to nitrogen oxides can also occur during electric arc welding, electroplating, and engraving. Engine exhaust contains nitrogen oxides, which are additionally produced when filling silos with grain containing high nitrite levels. Nitric oxide reacts with oxygen to form nitrogen dioxide.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Oxides of nitrogen can be produced by ice resurfacing machines ("Zamboni machines") during the combustion of their fuel source, propane. Exposure can be significant in indoor ice-skating rinks with poor ventilation. In 1987, 116 people attending an ice hockey game in Minnesota reported symptoms ranging from cough to hemoptysis due to substantial nitrogen dioxide exposure.</p>
        <p>The classic example of nitrogen dioxide toxicity is known as &#x0201c;silo filler&#x02019;s disease,&#x0201d; which occurs when nitrogen dioxide forms during the decomposition of silage and is subsequently inhaled.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r4">[4]</xref> Symptom severity depends on both the concentration of nitrogen dioxide and the duration of exposure. Initial significant exposure may result in shortness of breath, cough, or manifestations consistent with acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).&#x000a0;The manifestations&#x000a0;of nitrogen dioxide toxicity can also appear remotely from the work site due to delayed onset, necessitating careful history taking and monitoring after exposure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Inhalation of gases, aerosols, fumes, or dust can cause lung injury, asphyxiation, or systemic effects. The use of industrial chemicals that serve as sources of toxicity has increased. The National Occupational Exposure Survey estimates that more than 1 million US workers are at risk for exposure to pulmonary irritants annually. Employees in occupations with potential nitrogen oxide exposure are particularly susceptible to toxicity and may be unaware of ongoing exposures.</p>
        <p>Prolonged exposure permits higher concentrations of nitrogen oxides to reach the lower airways, resulting in delayed lower respiratory tract injury. Short-term nitrogen dioxide exposure has been associated with increased cardiovascular and respiratory mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>Nitrogen dioxide exposure from gas and propane stoves has been identified as a contributor to pediatric asthma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r9">[9]</xref> Exposure to ambient air pollutants is also a recognized cause of adult-onset asthma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Silo filler&#x02019;s disease is an occupational illness caused by inhalation of nitrogen oxides.&#x000a0;These substances form during the filling of agricultural grain silos with organic matter, typically grains like corn. The highest risk of overexposure occurs within the first month after silos are filled. Nitrogen oxides are heavier than air and accumulate above the silage. Exposure occurs when a worker enters a silo or opens the hatch without appropriate respiratory protection. Silo filler&#x02019;s disease is entirely preventable through proper workplace controls and the use of personal protective equipment. In some jurisdictions, confirmed cases require reporting to local public health authorities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Nitrogen oxides are irritant gases with low water solubility. In contrast, highly water-soluble gases deposit primarily in the upper airways, causing a rapid onset of upper airway and mucous membrane irritation. This early irritation typically prompts exposed individuals to remove themselves from the source. Gases with lower water solubility, such as nitrogen oxides, do not produce this immediate warning. Consequently, larger cumulative doses may be inhaled, and the gas can penetrate deeper into the respiratory tract. Low water solubility is a key factor in the delayed onset of pulmonary effects.</p>
        <p>Nitrogen dioxide and other nitrogen oxides damage lung tissue through the generation of reactive nitrogen-derived free radicals. Upon contact with water, nitrogen oxides produce nitric acid, which further irritates and damages pulmonary tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r13">[13]</xref> Low water solubility allows nitrogen oxides to reach distal bronchioles and adjacent alveoli. Nitrogen dioxide directly injures alveolar epithelial cells via toxic free radical formation, potentially resulting in diffuse alveolar damage.</p>
        <p>Increasing exposure concentrations raise the risk of pulmonary edema. Toxic pneumonitis and bronchiolitis may develop at levels of 25 to 100 ppm, whereas exposures above 150 ppm are usually fatal due to bronchiolitis obliterans, chemical pneumonitis, and pulmonary edema.</p>
        <p>Agents that penetrate the distal airways can produce persistent, chronic injuries such as bronchiolitis obliterans and bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia (BOOP). Bronchiolitis obliterans is characterized by narrowing and obstruction of the distal airways. Clinical presentation may include shortness of breath, cough, fever, and malaise. Chest radiographs may reveal hyperinflation, while pathology demonstrates granulation tissue, narrowed small airway lumens, and bronchiolar obliteration. Onset may be delayed. BOOP generally responds well to systemic corticosteroids. Pulmonary function testing often shows a restrictive pattern.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r14">[14]</xref> Lee et al described a patient with clinical, radiological, and pathological features of BOOP, likely a delayed consequence of nitrogen dioxide inhalation following a fire.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>The severity of lung injury depends on multiple factors. Increased risk and worse outcomes are observed in older adults,&#x000a0;very young patients, individuals with a history of allergic or nonallergic bronchospastic response, current or former smokers, and patients with underlying pulmonary disease, particularly reactive airway disease or conditions that impair host defense mechanisms.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The low water solubility of nitrogen dioxide allows prolonged exposure with minimal early warning. Initial symptoms may include a mild cough or nausea. Exposure to very high concentrations can produce upper respiratory irritation,&#x000a0;manifesting as burning eyes, sore throat, or cough. Chemical pneumonitis may develop up to 24 hours after exposure, presenting with progressive hypoxemia and pulmonary edema. Delayed complications can include bronchiolitis obliterans. Severe cases may manifest with features of ARDS, sepsis, or acute heart failure. A thorough exposure history is essential for accurate assessment.</p>
        <p>Nitrogen dioxide toxicity often follows a triphasic course. The 1st phase can present with cough, wheezing, dyspnea, central chest pain, fever, sweating, and weakness. Physical examination may reveal wheezing and crackles. Chest radiographs can be normal or demonstrate pulmonary edema. The 2nd phase is often asymptomatic. The 3rd phase occurs 2 to 8 weeks after exposure, with development of bronchiolitis obliterans. Clinical manifestations in this phase include fever, chills, wheezing, cough, dyspnea, and chest pain. Chest radiographs may be normal or show small, diffuse nodules.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Patients exposed to nitrogen dioxide require regular monitoring of vital signs and mental status. Continuous, invasive monitoring of oxygen saturation and end-tidal carbon dioxide is recommended to assess respiratory status. Diagnosis of nitrogen dioxide toxicity relies on a history of exposure, as no specific laboratory or radiographic test can confirm nitrogen dioxide inhalation. Evaluation of occupational or industrial environments may provide important exposure clues. Adjunctive testing in the emergency setting typically includes arterial blood gas analysis and chest radiography. After recovery from acute toxicity, pulmonary function testing may be indicated to evaluate residual&#x000a0;lung dysfunction.</p>
        <p>Arterial blood gas measurements can identify hypoxemia or low arterial partial pressure of oxygen. Nitrogen dioxide directly oxidizes oxyhemoglobin to a methemoglobin-peroxide complex. Chest radiographs may reveal patchy opacities. Pulmonary function testing can detect restrictive patterns or long-term complications, such as bronchiolitis obliterans.</p>
        <p>Methemoglobinemia should be considered in patients who are hypoxemic and cyanotic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r16">[16]</xref> Methemoglobin is hemoglobin in an oxidized form, and numerous oxidizing chemicals and drugs can induce methemoglobinemia, including nitrites and nitrates. This abnormal hemoglobin&#x000a0;cannot carry oxygen effectively, resulting in functional anemia. Diagnosis is suggested by the presence of dark brown or chocolate-colored blood, usually evident when methemoglobin exceeds 15%, and by hypoxemia unresponsive to supplemental oxygen. Arterial blood gas analysis typically shows a normal partial pressure of oxygen. Co-oximetry remains the gold standard for confirming the diagnosis.</p>
        <p>Clinical manifestations vary with methemoglobin concentration. Levels below 15% are usually asymptomatic. Concentrations of 15% to 20% can cause cyanosis with mild symptoms. Levels of 20% to 45% produce marked cyanosis and moderate symptoms. Concentrations of 45% to 50% result in&#x000a0;profound cyanosis and severe symptoms, whereas levels above 70% may be lethal. Treatment includes removal of the offending agent, aggressive delivery of supplemental oxygen, and administration of the antidote methylene blue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Significant inhalational exposures to&#x000a0;nitrogen oxides, such as nitrogen dioxide, require referral to an emergency department for evaluation. In the US, consultation with the local poison control center is recommended by calling 1-800-222-1222.</p>
        <p>Clinical evaluation of the nitrogen dioxide&#x02013;exposed patient should prioritize primary supportive measures, maintaining&#x000a0;a patent airway and supporting oxygenation, ventilation, and circulation as indicated. Immediate removal from the source of exposure is critical, with precautions to prevent additional casualties among rescue personnel.</p>
        <p>Supplemental oxygen is generally recommended following removal from exposure. Moving to an area with fresh, flowing air provides initial benefit. External decontamination is not typically necessary, and cross-contamination to health care personnel or other patients is not a concern. Even asymptomatic individuals require observation in a monitored setting for a minimum of 24 hours postexposure due to the risk of delayed chemical pneumonitis.</p>
        <p>Treatment of chemical pneumonitis and noncardiogenic pulmonary edema is primarily supportive, including the administration of supplemental oxygen, inhaled bronchodilators, inhaled corticosteroids, and mechanical ventilation as indicated. Corticosteroids have not demonstrated benefit in acute lung injury. Corticosteroid therapy may be appropriate in cases of delayed lung injury, such as bronchiolitis obliterans. One case report describes a patient with acute nitrogen dioxide exposure from electroplating who received intravenous methylprednisolone pulse therapy at 500 mg per day for 3 days early in hospitalization, resulting in marked improvement in respiratory status by day 5.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-43019.r19">[19]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis for delayed-onset pneumonitis following gaseous exposure includes&#x000a0;various conditions,&#x000a0;including the toxic inhalation of nitrogen oxides, chlorine gas, chloramine gas, ammonia, phosgene, sulfur oxides, hydrogen sulfide,&#x000a0;or cadmium, as well as metal fume fever from zinc oxide fumes. Any irritating gas has the potential to produce a similar clinical picture. Nonexposed patients with severe pneumonia, sepsis, acute heart failure, or any form of ARDS may present with symptoms indistinguishable from those caused by nitrogen dioxide exposure. Determination of the etiology relies on a thorough history of occupational and environmental exposures.</p>
        <p>Other conditions to consider in the differential diagnosis include ARDS, cardiogenic pulmonary edema, congestive heart failure with pulmonary edema, asthma, upper and lower respiratory tract infections, methemoglobinemia, farmer&#x02019;s lung, smoke inhalation, carbon monoxide toxicity, miliary tuberculosis, chlorine gas toxicity, hydrogen sulfide toxicity, and phosgene toxicity.&#x000a0;Implementation of structured exposure assessment, risk-stratified monitoring, and prompt supportive care can reduce morbidity from nitrogen dioxide inhalation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Prognosis depends on the intensity, duration, and frequency of nitrogen dioxide exposure. Most patients achieve full recovery following an initial acute exposure if adequate supportive care is provided. Severe cases may require extensive critical care support,&#x000a0;which may include mechanical ventilation and extracorporeal membrane oxygenation, to sustain respiratory function during acute toxicity and allow time for pulmonary healing and recovery.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>A 3rd phase may develop 2 to 8 weeks following the initial acute phase of chemical pneumonitis, characterized by the onset of bronchiolitis obliterans. Clinical manifestations can include fever, chills, wheezing, cough, dyspnea, or chest pain. Chest radiographs may be normal or demonstrate small, diffuse nodules. Anecdotal evidence suggests that systemic corticosteroids may provide therapeutic benefit during this stage.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s12" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Patients with significant nitrogen dioxide exposure require admission to a monitored setting for a minimum of 24 hours. Consultation with the local poison control center or a medical toxicologist is recommended. Involvement of a critical care physician or a pulmonary medicine specialist may also be appropriate.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Prevention of nitrogen dioxide exposure relies on adherence to established occupational and environmental standards. The American Conference of Governmental Industrial Hygienists recommends a time-weighted average of 3 ppm and a short-term exposure limit of 5 ppm for occupational settings. The National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health advises a permissible exposure limit of 5 ppm.</p>
        <p>Environmental regulations also provide guidance for the general population. The US Environmental Protection Agency maintains a national ambient air quality standard with a 1-hour maximum daily concentration of 100 ppb and an annual standard of 53 ppb. The World Health Organization recommends an annual mean of 40 mcg/m3 and a 1-hour mean of 200 mcg/m3. Patient education should emphasize the importance of minimizing exposure through proper ventilation, use of&#x000a0;personal protective equipment in occupational or high-risk environments, and awareness of local air quality standards to prevent acute and chronic respiratory injury.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Nitrogen dioxide exposure can occur from filling or disturbing stored silage in grain silos, operating ice-resurfacing machines such as "Zamboni" machines, electroplating, or burning nitrogen-based fuel. Patients with acute exposures require admission for a minimum of 24 hours to monitor for delayed-onset chemical pneumonitis. Awareness of nitrogen dioxide&#x02019;s triphasic illness is essential, with careful assessment for signs and symptoms 2 to 8 weeks after initial exposure.&#x000a0;Management focuses on immediate removal from the exposure source and supportive care for acute lung injury.&#x000a0;Systemic corticosteroids may be considered in cases of delayed lung injury, such as in bronchiolitis obliterans. Prompt recognition of occupational or environmental risk factors and structured follow-up can prevent progression and improve clinical outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-43019.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Patients exposed to nitrogen dioxide may present with nonspecific signs of airway irritation or&#x000a0;be initially asymptomatic. A thorough occupational and environmental exposure history is critical for accurate diagnosis and initiation of appropriate treatment. Nitrogen dioxide exposure can result in significant morbidity and mortality due to delayed and chronic pulmonary effects. Emergency physicians are typically involved in the initial care of acutely exposed patients. Consultation with a medical toxicologist is essential in all suspected or confirmed cases. Pulmonologists or critical care physicians should be engaged for the management of lung injury or prolonged complications. Local public health authorities may also participate in the investigation of workplace exposures.</p>
        <p>Care is delivered by an interprofessional team beginning with emergency medical technicians, triage nurses, and emergency department personnel. Toxicology pharmacists or clinicians assist with pharmaceutical management, including medication reconciliation, dosing verification, and communication of concerns to treating clinicians. Critical care nurses monitor patients, administer therapy, and facilitate coordination among team members.</p>
      </sec>
